\title{Weakly $D_6$-Free Families in the Boolean Lattice:\\
an Abelian No-Go Theorem, a Tur\'an Ceiling,\\ and the Interval Hierarchy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 7, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $2^{[n]}$ be the Boolean lattice of subsets of $[n]=\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, ordered by inclusion. A family $\Fam\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ \emph{weakly contains} the $j$-diamond $D_j$ if there are $A,C\in\Fam$ and $j$ distinct $B_1,\dots,B_j\in\Fam$ with $A\subsetneq B_i\subsetneq C$; otherwise $\Fam$ is \emph{weakly $D_j$-free}. Three consecutive layers form a weakly $D_6$-free family of size $\binom{n}{k}+\binom{n}{k+1}+\binom{n}{k+2}\approx 3\central$. We study whether a fixed rational constant $c>3$ is attainable by the natural mechanism of thinning a fourth layer with an algebraic labelling. We prove: (i) the three-layer family is saturated and four full layers are never free; (ii) inside a four-layer window, weak $D_6$-freeness is \emph{equivalent} to a deterministic interval-exclusion condition, via the exact count $|(A,C)|=2^{|C|-|A|}-2$; (iii) if the interior layer is thinned by a sum-labelling $v:[n]\to V$ into a finite abelian group with keep-set $U$, then freeness forces an algebraic safety condition, safety forces every label class to have at most two points, and (once the labels occupy more than two thirds of $V$, automatic when $4|V|<3n$) forces $|U|\le 2$; consequently $|\Fam|\le 3\central+2b$ and $c\le 3+2/K$ whenever $Kb\le\central$, i.e.\ $c\le 3+O(1/n)$ --- \emph{no constant improvement is possible by an abelian sum-labelling}; and (iv) link graphs of weakly $D_6$-free families are triangle-free, so Mantel's theorem and double counting give $4\binom{k+2}{2}|\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k{+}2)|\le\binom{n}{k}(n-k)^2$, capping four-layer constructions near $3.5\central$. All results are the case $m=3$ of a hierarchy in which, in a window of $m+1$ layers, weak $D_{2^m-1}$-freeness is automatic and weak $D_{2^m-2}$-freeness is equivalent to height-$m$ interval exclusion.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Extremal set theory asks for the maximum size of a family $\Fam\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ avoiding a prescribed subposet. The \emph{diamond} $D_j$ has a minimum, a maximum, and $j$ middle elements. Throughout we use \emph{weak} containment: $\Fam$ weakly contains $D_j$ if it contains $A\subsetneq C$ and $j$ distinct sets strictly between them, with no condition on how those middle sets relate to one another. Weak freeness is therefore the stronger property.

The standard lower bound comes from consecutive layers. If all members of $\Fam$ have sizes in a window of $m+1$ consecutive values, any interval between two members has height at most $m$ and hence at most $2^m-2$ interior sets. Since $2^2-2=2<3$, three consecutive layers are weakly $D_3$-free, giving families of size $\approx 3\central$ that avoid $D_j$ for all $j\ge3$.

For $j=6$ the window $m=3$ is on the verge of usability: an interval of height $3$ has exactly $2^3-2=6$ interior sets, so a four-layer family is weakly $D_6$-free precisely when it never contains all six. This near-miss motivates:

\begin{conjecture}[the target]
There exist explicit integers $q$, $n_0$ and a rational $c>3$ such that for all $n\ge n_0$ a labelling of $[n]$ into a finite vector space over $\mathbb{F}_q$ produces a weakly $D_6$-free family in $2^{[n]}$ of size at least $c\central$.
\end{conjecture}

The strategic idea is that interval exclusion is \emph{deterministic}, so only the lower bound on the number of selected sets requires quantitative estimates.

\subsection{Contribution}

We carry out the deterministic half of the program in full and, in doing so, refute the conjecture for the entire class of abelian sum-labelling constructions: the deterministic condition is a characterisation (Theorem~\ref{thm:excl}); for sum-labellings it is exactly an algebraic safety condition (Theorem~\ref{thm:safe}); safety allows at most two label points per class (Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}) and at most two surviving colours (Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}); hence the gain over three layers is $O(1/n)$ relative to $\central$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:gain}); and independently, extremal graph theory caps all four-layer constructions keeping three layers whole near $3.5\central$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:turan}).

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{definition}[Layer]
$\Lay(n,k)=\{S\subseteq[n]:|S|=k\}$, so $|\Lay(n,k)|=\binom nk$. Distinct layers are disjoint.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Weak diamond containment]
$\Fam$ \emph{weakly contains} $D_j$ if there are $A,C\in\Fam$ and $\mathcal{S}\subseteq\Fam$ with $|\mathcal{S}|=j$ and $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ for all $B\in\mathcal{S}$. Otherwise $\Fam$ is \emph{weakly $D_j$-free}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]
If $j\le l$ then weak $D_j$-freeness implies weak $D_l$-freeness.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Discarding $l-j$ middle sets turns a weak $D_l$ into a weak $D_j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Open interval]
$(A,C)=\{B: A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Interval size]\label{thm:interval}
If $A\subseteq C$ and $A\neq C$ then $|(A,C)|=2^{|C|-|A|}-2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The maps $B\mapsto B\setminus A$ and $D\mapsto A\cup D$ are mutually inverse bijections between $(A,C)$ and $\{D\subseteq C\setminus A: D\neq\varnothing,\ D\neq C\setminus A\}$. Indeed $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ forces $A\subseteq B\subseteq C$; strictness at the bottom says $B\setminus A\neq\varnothing$ and at the top says $B\setminus A\neq C\setminus A$. The target set has $2^{|C\setminus A|}-2$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Height of a diamond]\label{lem:height}
If $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ then $A\subseteq C$ and $|C|\ge|A|+2$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The three-layer baseline and the exclusion criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Three layers are weakly $D_3$-free]\label{thm:base}
If every $S\in\Fam$ satisfies $k\le|S|\le k+2$, then $\Fam$ is weakly $D_3$-free, hence weakly $D_6$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $A,C\in\Fam$ have three distinct members of $\Fam$ strictly between them. By Lemma~\ref{lem:height}, $|C|\ge|A|+2$; the window forces $|C|-|A|=2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:interval} the interval $(A,C)$ has $2^2-2=2$ elements, which cannot contain three distinct sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Baseline family]
$\mathcal{T}(n,k)=\Lay(n,k)\cup\Lay(n,k{+}1)\cup\Lay(n,k{+}2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Baseline cardinality]
$|\mathcal{T}(n,k)|=\binom nk+\binom n{k+1}+\binom n{k+2}$ and $\mathcal{T}(n,k)$ is weakly $D_6$-free. For $k=\lfloor n/2\rfloor-1$ this is $(3-o(1))\central$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Interval-exclusion criterion]\label{thm:excl}
Let $\Fam$ satisfy $k\le|S|\le k+3$ for all $S\in\Fam$. Then $\Fam$ is weakly $D_6$-free if and only if
\begin{equation}\tag{E}\label{eq:E}
\forall A,C\in\Fam \text{ with } A\subseteq C,\ |C|=|A|+3:\quad \exists B,\ A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C,\ B\notin\Fam .
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Necessity.} If \eqref{eq:E} fails for a height-$3$ pair $A\subseteq C$, then $(A,C)\subseteq\Fam$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:interval} this interval has exactly six elements, which witness a weak $D_6$.

\emph{Sufficiency.} Assume \eqref{eq:E} and suppose six distinct members of $\Fam$ lie strictly between $A,C\in\Fam$. They lie in $(A,C)$, so $6\le 2^{|C|-|A|}-2$, giving $|C|-|A|\ge3$; the window gives $|C|-|A|\le3$. Hence $|(A,C)|=6$ and the six middle sets exhaust $(A,C)$, so $(A,C)\subseteq\Fam$, contradicting \eqref{eq:E}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A full interval is a weak $D_6$]\label{thm:full}
If $A,C\in\Fam$, $A\subseteq C$, $|C|=|A|+3$ and $(A,C)\subseteq\Fam$, then $\Fam$ is not weakly $D_6$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Four full layers are never free]
For $k+3\le n$, the family $\bigcup_{j=0}^{3}\Lay(n,k{+}j)$ is not weakly $D_6$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $A$ of size $k$ and extend it to $C\subseteq[n]$ of size $k+3$. Every $B$ with $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ has $|B|\in\{k+1,k+2\}$ and so lies in the family; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:full}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation of the baseline]\label{thm:sat}
Let $X\subseteq[n]$. If $|X|=k+3$, then $\{X\}\cup\mathcal{T}(n,k)$ is not weakly $D_6$-free. If $|X|=k$ and $k+3\le n$, then $\{X\}\cup\mathcal{T}(n,k{+}1)$ is not weakly $D_6$-free. Hence $\mathcal{T}(n,k)$ is maximal weakly $D_6$-free inside its four-layer window.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In the first case pick $A\subseteq X$ with $|A|=k$; the six sets strictly between $A$ and $X$ have sizes $k+1,k+2$ and lie in $\mathcal{T}(n,k)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:full}. The second case is symmetric, extending $X$ upward to a $(k+3)$-set.
\end{proof}

\section{The abelian no-go theorem}

\begin{definition}[Sum labelling and selected family]
Let $V$ be a finite abelian group, $v:[n]\to V$, $U\subseteq V$. Set $\sigma_v(S)=\sum_{i\in S}v(i)$ and
\[
\Fam(n,k,v,U)=\Lay(n,k)\cup\Lay(n,k{+}1)\cup\{S\in\Lay(n,k{+}2):\sigma_v(S)\in U\}\cup\Lay(n,k{+}3).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sum-richness]
$v$ is \emph{sum-rich at level $k$} if for every $g\in V$ and all $x,y,z\in[n]$ there is a $k$-set $A\subseteq[n]$ with $A\cap\{x,y,z\}=\varnothing$ and $\sigma_v(A)=g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Safety]
$(v,U)$ is \emph{safe} if there is no $g\in V$ and no three distinct $x,y,z\in[n]$ with
\[
g+v(x)+v(y)\in U,\qquad g+v(x)+v(z)\in U,\qquad g+v(y)+v(z)\in U.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Freeness forces safety]\label{thm:safe}
If $v$ is sum-rich at level $k$ and $\Fam(n,k,v,U)$ is weakly $D_6$-free, then $(v,U)$ is safe.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose safety fails, witnessed by $g$ and distinct $x,y,z\in[n]$. By sum-richness choose a $k$-set $A$ disjoint from $\{x,y,z\}$ with $\sigma_v(A)=g$, and put $C=A\cup\{x,y,z\}$, of size $k+3$. Both $A$ and $C$ lie in $\Fam$, since layers $k$ and $k+3$ are kept whole. By Theorem~\ref{thm:excl} some $B$ with $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ is missing from $\Fam$. Such a $B$ equals $A\cup D$ with $\varnothing\neq D\subsetneq\{x,y,z\}$, so $|B|\in\{k+1,k+2\}$. If $|B|=k+1$ then $B\in\Lay(n,k{+}1)\subseteq\Fam$. If $|B|=k+2$ then $D$ is a pair $\{a,b\}\subseteq\{x,y,z\}$ and, $A$ and $D$ being disjoint, $\sigma_v(B)=g+v(a)+v(b)\in U$ by the failure of safety, so again $B\in\Fam$. Both cases contradict the choice of $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Label multiplicity]\label{thm:fibre}
If $(v,U)$ is safe and $U\neq\varnothing$ then $|v^{-1}(a)\cap[n]|\le2$ for every $a\in V$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $x,y,z\in[n]$ are distinct with $v(x)=v(y)=v(z)=a$, pick $u\in U$ and set $g=u-2a$. All three pair-labels equal $g+a+a=u\in U$, contradicting safety.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:Vlarge}
If $U\neq\varnothing$ then $|v([n])|\ge n/2$ and hence $|V|\ge n/2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The closure obstruction]\label{thm:closure}
Suppose $(v,U)$ is safe and the label set $L=v([n])$ satisfies $2|V|<3|L|$. Then $|U|\le2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $u_1,u_2,u_3\in U$ are distinct. Put $\alpha=u_1-u_3$ and $\beta=u_1-u_2$; both are nonzero and $\alpha\neq\beta$. Translation is a bijection, so $|L|=|L-\alpha|=|L-\beta|>\tfrac23|V|$, and two applications of $|X|+|Y|\le|X\cap Y|+|V|$ give
\[
|L\cap(L-\alpha)\cap(L-\beta)|\ \ge\ 3|L|-2|V|\ >\ 0 .
\]
Pick $c$ in the triple intersection; then $c,\,c+\alpha,\,c+\beta\in L$, so there are $z,x,y\in[n]$ with $v(z)=c$, $v(x)=c+\alpha$, $v(y)=c+\beta$. Since $\alpha,\beta,\alpha-\beta\neq0$, these labels are pairwise distinct and so are $x,y,z$. Set $g=u_2+u_3-u_1-2c$. Then
\[
g+v(x)+v(y)=u_1,\qquad g+v(x)+v(z)=u_2,\qquad g+v(y)+v(z)=u_3,
\]
all in $U$, contradicting safety.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Small groups cannot help]\label{thm:small}
If $(v,U)$ is safe, $U\neq\varnothing$ and $4|V|<3n$, then $|U|\le2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre} each fibre of $v$ has at most two points, so $n\le2|L|$ and $|L|\ge n/2>\tfrac23|V|$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality ceiling]\label{thm:cardceil}
Suppose $|\{S\in\Lay(n,k{+}2):\sigma_v(S)=u\}|\le b$ for all $u\in V$, and $|U|\le2$. Then
\[
|\Fam(n,k,v,U)|\ \le\ 3\central+2b .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The thinned layer is contained in $|U|\le2$ colour classes, each of size at most $b$. Each of the three full layers has at most $\central$ members, since the central binomial coefficient dominates every layer.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No constant improvement]\label{thm:gain}
Let $v$ be sum-rich at level $k$ with $2|V|<3|v([n])|$, let every interior colour class have at most $b$ members, and let $K$ be a positive integer with $Kb\le\central$. If $\Fam(n,k,v,U)$ is weakly $D_6$-free then
\[
K\,|\Fam(n,k,v,U)|\ \le\ (3K+2)\central,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad |\Fam|\le\Bigl(3+\tfrac2K\Bigr)\central .
\]
The same holds with $2|V|<3|v([n])|$ replaced by $4|V|<3n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:safe}, then Theorem~\ref{thm:closure} (or Theorem~\ref{thm:small}) to get $|U|\le2$, then Theorem~\ref{thm:cardceil}; multiply by $K$ and use $Kb\le\central$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Refutation for the abelian mechanism]\label{cor:refute}
Under the equidistribution $b\approx\binom{n}{k+2}/|V|$ and $|V|\ge n/2$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:Vlarge}), taking $K\approx|V|$ gives
\[
c\ \le\ 3+\frac{2}{|V|}\ \le\ 3+\frac4n\ =\ 3+O(1/n).
\]
Hence for every fixed rational $c>3$ and every finite abelian group $V$ the labelling mechanism fails for all large $n$: the gain is positive but vanishing.
\end{corollary}

\section{A Tur\'an ceiling for four-layer constructions}

\begin{definition}[Link graph]
For $A\in\Lay(n,k)$, the link graph $G_A$ of $\Fam$ has vertex set $[n]\setminus A$ and edges $\{x,y\}$ with $A\cup\{x,y\}\in\Fam$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Link graphs are triangle-free]\label{thm:trifree}
Let $\Fam$ be weakly $D_6$-free with $\Lay(n,k{+}1)\subseteq\Fam$ and $\Lay(n,k{+}3)\subseteq\Fam$, and let $A\in\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k)$. Then $G_A$ has no triangle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\{x,y\},\{x,z\},\{y,z\}$ are edges, put $C=A\cup\{x,y,z\}\in\Lay(n,k{+}3)\subseteq\Fam$. The interval $(A,C)$ consists of three $(k{+}1)$-sets, present since layer $k+1$ is full, and three $(k{+}2)$-sets, present by the triangle. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:full}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Mantel bound for links]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:trifree}, $4|E(G_A)|\le(n-k)^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$G_A$ is triangle-free on $n-k$ vertices, so Mantel's theorem (the $r=2$ case of Tur\'an's theorem) bounds $|E(G_A)|$ by $\lfloor(n-k)^2/4\rfloor$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tur\'an ceiling for the interior layer]\label{thm:turan}
Let $\Fam$ be weakly $D_6$-free with $\Lay(n,k),\Lay(n,k{+}1),\Lay(n,k{+}3)\subseteq\Fam$. Then
\[
4\binom{k+2}{2}\bigl|\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k{+}2)\bigr|\ \le\ \binom nk (n-k)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Double count incidences $(A,S)$ with $A\in\Lay(n,k)$, $S\in\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k{+}2)$ and $A\subseteq S$. For fixed $A$ the sets $S$ above it correspond bijectively to edges of $G_A$ via $S\mapsto S\setminus A$, of which there are at most $(n-k)^2/4$; there are $\binom nk$ choices of $A$. For fixed $S$ the sets $A$ below it are the $k$-subsets of $S$, of which there are $\binom{k+2}{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Partial-layer refinement]
If $\Fam$ is weakly $D_6$-free and contains layers $k+1$ and $k+3$ in full, then
\[
4\sum_{S\in\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k+2)}\bigl|\{A\in\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k):A\subseteq S\}\bigr|\ \le\ \bigl|\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k)\bigr|(n-k)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total ceiling]
If in addition every member of $\Fam$ has size in $[k,k+3]$, then
\[
4\binom{k+2}{2}|\Fam|\ \le\ 4\binom{k+2}{2}\Bigl(\binom nk+\binom n{k+1}+\binom n{k+3}\Bigr)+\binom nk (n-k)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The $3.5$ corridor]
For $k\approx(n-3)/2$ one has $(n-k)^2\approx n^2/4$ and $4\binom{k+2}{2}\approx n^2/2$, so at most about half the interior layer survives and $|\Fam|\lesssim3.5\central$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The height hierarchy}

Nothing above depends on the number $6$ except through $6=2^3-2$.

\begin{theorem}[Automatic freeness]
If $m\ge1$ and every member of $\Fam$ has size in $[k,k+m]$, then $\Fam$ is weakly $D_{2^m-1}$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Any interval between two members has height at most $m$, hence at most $2^m-2<2^m-1$ interior sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Critical criterion]
Let $m\ge2$ and let every member of $\Fam$ have size in $[k,k+m]$. Then $\Fam$ is weakly $D_{2^m-2}$-free if and only if every $A,C\in\Fam$ with $A\subseteq C$ and $|C|=|A|+m$ admit some $B$ with $A\subsetneq B\subsetneq C$ and $B\notin\Fam$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A complete height-$m$ interval provides $2^m-2$ middle sets. Conversely $2^m-2$ middle sets force $2^m-2\le2^{|C|-|A|}-2$, hence height exactly $m$, hence the middle sets exhaust the interval.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Window cardinality and saturation]
The window $\Win(n,k,m)=\bigcup_{j<m}\Lay(n,k{+}j)$ has $\sum_{j<m}\binom{n}{k+j}$ members, and for $m\ge2$ it is saturated: for every $X\subseteq[n]$ with $|X|=k+m$, the family $\{X\}\cup\Win(n,k,m)$ is not weakly $D_{2^m-2}$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $A\subseteq X$ with $|A|=k$. Every $B$ strictly between $A$ and $X$ has $k<|B|<k+m$, hence lies in the window, so the height-$m$ interval $(A,X)$ is complete and contributes $2^m-2$ middle sets.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and numerical verification}

\paragraph{Exhaustive weak-diamond detection.} For each comparable pair $A\subseteq C$ in $\Fam$ with $|C|\ge|A|+2$, test whether $|(A,C)\cap\Fam|\ge j$. In a window of height $m$ the inner enumeration is over $2^m-2$ candidates, giving $O(|\Fam|^2 2^m)$ total.

\paragraph{Interval-exclusion certification.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:excl}, for four-layer families it suffices to enumerate pairs $(A,T)$ with $A\in\Fam\cap\Lay(n,k)$ and $T$ a $3$-subset of $[n]\setminus A$, and check that one of the six sets $A\cup D$, $\varnothing\neq D\subsetneq T$, is absent. Cost $O\!\left(\binom nk\binom{n-k}3\right)$ with constant $6$.

\paragraph{Safety search.} $(v,U)$ is unsafe iff for some ordered triple $u_1,u_2,u_3\in U$ with $\alpha=u_1-u_3$, $\beta=u_1-u_2$, the intersection $L\cap(L-\alpha)\cap(L-\beta)$ is nonempty, where $L=v([n])$. Cost $O(|U|^3|V|+n)$.

\paragraph{Gain evaluation.} With $N_u=|\{S\in\Lay(n,k{+}2):\sigma_v(S)=u\}|$, the selected family has size $\binom nk+\binom n{k+1}+\binom n{k+3}+\sum_{u\in U}N_u$; dividing by $\central$ gives the empirical constant. Numerically, the maximal safe keep-set has two elements and $c-3$ tracks $2/|V|$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the results mean for the conjecture}
The conjecture bet on a separation of concerns: exclusion is deterministic, so only the size of the selection needs quantitative work. Theorem~\ref{thm:safe} vindicates that half completely --- the translation from combinatorics to algebra is exact. But the deterministic condition is far stronger than anticipated: safety forbids all but two colours, so no choice of $q$, of dimension, or of $U$ yields a constant gain.

\subsection{Where the argument is tight}
Two features drive the collapse. \emph{Translation invariance}: the shift $g$ is free because $\sigma_v(A\cup D)=\sigma_v(A)+\sigma_v(D)$ and $\sigma_v(A)$ ranges over $V$. \emph{Pair symmetry}: the three interior $(k{+}2)$-sets differ from $A$ by the three pairs of a triple, and $v(x)+v(y)$ is symmetric. Breaking either feature is the natural escape route.

\subsection{Consequences for the search space}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item Improvements must delete from interior layers only (Theorem~\ref{thm:sat}).
\item The deletion rule is characterised by a local deterministic condition (Theorem~\ref{thm:excl}), so no probabilistic construction beats the best deterministic one.
\item The rule cannot be an abelian sum-labelling (Theorem~\ref{thm:gain}, Corollary~\ref{cor:refute}).
\item Four-layer constructions keeping three layers whole cap at $c\le3.5$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:turan}).
\end{enumerate}
The corridor is therefore $3<c\le3.5$.

\subsection{Future directions}
\emph{Bipartite-link threshold constructions.} Fix $X\subseteq[n]$ with $|X|=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and keep a $(k{+}2)$-set $S$ by a rule depending only on $|S\cap X|$. Keeping only ``split'' added pairs makes every link graph complete bipartite, hence triangle-free by construction --- exactly the extremal shape for Mantel's theorem.

\emph{Nonabelian and nonlinear labellings.} Replace $\sigma_v$ by an ordered product in a nonabelian group, a quadratic form, or a bounded-depth Boolean threshold, so as to destroy the free shift parameter that powers the closure obstruction.

\emph{Higher windows.} For $m\ge4$ the relevant threshold is $D_{2^m-2}$ and the baseline is $m$ layers; the number of intervals grows faster than the number of sets, so deletion is cheaper per interval broken.

\emph{Sharpening the ceiling.} Link graphs at nested bottoms are highly correlated, so a stability or supersaturation form of Mantel's theorem should push the constant below $3.5$.

\emph{Two-sided thinning.} Thinning two interior layers turns the problem into a covering problem: cover all height-$3$ intervals by removed sets with minimum cost. Its LP relaxation, exactly computable for small $n$, would reveal whether any four-layer construction can beat $3$ by a constant.

\section{Conclusion}

The identity $|(A,C)|=2^{|C|-|A|}-2$ organises the whole theory: it yields the three-layer baseline and its saturation, the exact interval-exclusion criterion in a four-layer window, the height hierarchy in which weak $D_{2^m-2}$-freeness corresponds to exclusion at height $m$, and --- through the induced safety condition --- a complete refutation of abelian sum-labelling as a route to a constant improvement. Together with the Mantel ceiling near $3.5$, the question ``is there a constant $c>3$?'' now has a precise shape: it lives in the corridor $3<c\le3.5$ and demands a deletion rule outside the abelian world.

